.TH SET_INSERTS 3 "15 Mar 2023" "CB" "Library Functions"

.SH NAME
set_inserts \- Insert a string into a set

.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB #include <lib/set.h>
.IB #include <lib/str.h>

.IB int
.IB set_inserts(set_t* set, const char* str);

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBset_inserts\fR function inserts a string into a set. It takes two arguments: the set and the string. The function returns 1 if the string is successfully inserted, 2 if the string already exists in the set, and -1 if an error occurs.

.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.IB set
The set into which to insert the string.
.TP
.IB str
The string to insert.

.SH RETURN VALUE
The function returns 1 if the string is successfully inserted, 2 if the string already exists in the set, and -1 if an error occurs.

.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates how to use the \fBset_inserts\fR function:

.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <lib/set.h>
#include <lib/str.h>

int main() {
    set_t set;
    const char* str = "apple";

    set_init(&set);
    if (set_inserts(&set, str) == 1) {
        printf("String inserted successfully\n");
    } else if (set_inserts(&set, str) == 2) {
        printf("String already exists in set\n");
    } else {
        printf("Error inserting string\n");
    }

    set_free(&set);
    return 0;
}
.nef